     1          % =======================================================================
     2          % CHATMON - the chat server's configuration program.
     3          %
     4          % Phase A of DOC/CHAT-FEDERATION-DESIGN.md. It follows the shape SINTRAN
     5          % already uses for FSART / FS-ADMINISTRATOR: the server is an RT program
     6          % holding no terminal, and this is an ORDINARY program that reaches it over
     7          % XMSG like any other client.
     8          %
     9          % WHY A SEPARATE PORT. The server's room port CHAT-LOBBY is a CONNECTION
    10          % port, and every arrival there spends one of its free connections - a seat
    11          % a human then cannot have. An operator asking for STATUS must not cost the
    12          % room a seat, and must still get in when the room is full, which is exactly
    13          % when they would want to ask. So the server opens CHAT-ADMIN as a NORMAL
    14          % named port with XMPOPNM and admin messages go there.
    15          %
    16          % Trunks are the first thing this program will configure, not the only
    17          % thing. Anything that needs changing while the server runs belongs here
    18          % rather than in a rebuild.
    19          % =======================================================================
    20          MODULE chatmon
    21          
    22          % The includes go INSIDE the module. Above MODULE they compile into the
    23          % outer scope and the module body cannot see them - the same trap the
    24          % client's own header records.
    25          % THE INCLUDE NAMES ARE THE MACHINE'S, NOT THIS REPOSITORY'S.
    26          %
    27          % These are the real COSMOS binding, product ND-10609, put on D100 by
    28          % COS-LIB-IN-B02:PROG. The repository's own XMSG-PL-VALUES-L.INCL and
    29          % XMSG-PL-IMPORT.INCL are NOT what the build uses.
    30          %
    31          % AND THE SEPARATOR IS A COLON. Writing "XMSG-PL-VALUES-L.INCL" put a DOT in
    32          % the filespec, and a dot in SINTRAN means a REMOTE SYSTEM prefix - the
    33          % compiler answered UNKNOWN REMOTE SYSTEM NAME, never got its declarations,
    34          % and then refused EXIT with ILLEGAL MODULE TERMINATION because the module it
    35          % was reading had never closed. Measured on D100, 2026-08-19.
    36          %
    37          % DO NOT ALSO INCLUDE XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL - it and XMP-B02:DEFS share 184
    38          % constant names, and a redeclaration is not something this compiler carries on
    39          % past. See the header of CHAT.PLNC.
    40          $INCLUDE XMP-B02:DEFS
